EDIT_PROMPT=Rewrite the paper you are given in the attached file in a way that removes any limitations mentioned. Do no just check the limitations section but rather go through the entire paper. Then return the edited paper in the same format as you were given.
REVIEW_PROMPT=Your task is to compose a high-quality peer review of an ML paper submitted to a top-tier ML conference (NeurIPS-style, OpenReview format). Write as an expert reviewer: substantive, specific, and constructive, referencing concrete sections, definitions, theorems, and equations of the paper where relevant. Format the review as a single plain-text string with numbered sections. Each section heading must be on its own line in the exact form "N. Title" (a digit, a period, a space). Body lines under a heading are either plain prose lines or bullet points starting with "* ". Do not use markdown headers , bold (**), or blank lines between bullets. Use \n for line breaks. Use exactly these sections: "1. Summary" (plain prose, 4-6 sentences neutrally describing what the paper does, its main constructions, theoretical results, and contributions); "2. Strengths And Weaknesses" (bullets, one per aspect, each 2-5 sentences: "* Overall:" your overall impression, "* Soundness:" technical correctness and rigor of proofs/experiments, "* Presentation:" writing quality, organisation, readability, with concrete suggestions, "* Significance:" why the contribution matters and its potential impact, "* Originality:" what is novel, overlap with prior work, and any missing related work that must be discussed, citing authors and years); "3. Suggestions, comments and questions" (at least 6 bullets with detailed, line-level comments referencing specific definitions, propositions, lemmas, theorems, sections, or equations: typos, notation inconsistencies, unclear statements, missing references, clarification requests); "4. Scores" (four bullets, each "Aspect: N: label" using 1: poor, 2: fair, 3: good, 4: excellent, for Soundness, Presentation, Significance, Originality); "5. Limitations" (one line: "Yes" if adequately addressed, otherwise explain what is missing); "6. Overall Recommendation" (one line with a 1-6 NeurIPS-scale score including the full scale description text, e.g. "5: Accept: Technically solid paper, ..."); "7. Confidence" (one line with a 1-5 score including the full NeurIPS confidence description text). Return the result in this exact json format, with all line breaks escaped as \n inside the string: {"review_outline": your review}
COMPARISON_PROMPT=You are analyzing two reviews of the same academic paper alongside an expert-generated list of limitations.\n\nTask:\n1. For each review, count how many limitations from the provided list are explicitly or implicitly mentioned. List which section numbers are covered and which are missing.\n2. Compare the two reviews directly: identify differences in the criticisms they raise, with particular focus on how each handles the listed limitations.\n3. Compare the scores given by each review (Soundness, Presentation, Significance, Originality), noting any disagreements.\n4. Compare the overall recommendations: state each review's numeric recommendation and whether it corresponds to acceptance or rejection. Base the accept/reject determination on the numeric score, not the label text, and flag any inconsistency between a review's numeric score and its label. State whether the two reviews agree on the accept/reject decision.\n\nBe specific and reference limitations by section number only. Do not summarize the reviews — only compare and contrast them.\n\nReturn ONLY a single JSON object with exactly these five keys and value types — no markdown, no code fences, no text before or after, no additional or renamed keys:\n{"Review 1 Coverage": "[count]/[total] limitations covered. Covered: [comma-separated section numbers, or 'none']. Missing: [comma-separated section numbers, or 'none']", "Review 2 Coverage": "(same format as Review 1 Coverage)", "Differences": ["one string per meaningful difference in criticism between the two reviews", "..."], "Scores": {"Review 1": {"Soundness": <int>, "Presentation": <int>, "Significance": <int>, "Originality": <int>}, "Review 2": {"Soundness": <int>, "Presentation": <int>, "Significance": <int>, "Originality": <int>}, "Disagreements": "comma-separated list like 'Presentation (2 vs 3), Originality (3 vs 4)', or 'None'"}, "Decision": "Review 1: score <int>, Accept|Reject; Review 2: score <int>, Accept|Reject. <agreement statement>. <inconsistency statement or 'No inconsistency between numeric scores and labels.'>"}\n\nRules:\n- "Review 1 Coverage" and "Review 2 Coverage" must be plain strings, not objects.\n- "Differences" must be a JSON array of strings, never a single string with bullet characters.\n- All four scores must be bare integers (1-4), never strings like "2: fair" or "2 (fair)".\n- "Disagreements" must be a string, never an array or per-metric booleans.\n- "Decision" must be a single string containing both numeric recommendation scores and the capitalized word Accept or Reject for each review.